---
title: Defining a query handler
description: Writing the handler that answers a query, streaming a sequence, and dispatching both.
---

A query has exactly one handler, found through the contract it implements.

## A single result

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class GetProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public async ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = await repository.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
        return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

## A stream

The method is `StreamAsync`, and it takes the query and the context:

```csharp
public sealed class SearchProductsHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IStreamQueryHandler<SearchProducts, ProductDto>
{
    public async IAsyncEnumerable<ProductDto> StreamAsync(
        SearchProducts query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        await foreach (var product in repository.SearchAsync(query.Term, context.CancellationToken))
            yield return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

Cancellation comes from `context.CancellationToken`, so there is no separate token parameter to
forget — pass it into whatever you enumerate.

{% aside type="note" %}
A `null` element is a legitimate element. If your sequence is `IAsyncEnumerable<ProductDto?>`
and you yield `null`, the caller receives it — the pipeline does not filter nulls out.
{% /aside %}

## Dispatching

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id), ct);
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id), GroupSet.Of("cached"), ct);
```

And a stream:

```csharp
await foreach (var product in mediator.StreamAsync(new SearchProducts("bench"))
                                      .WithCancellation(ct))
{
    …
}
```

When both types are known at the call site, naming them keeps the dispatch from constructing its
executor reflectively — which matters for NativeAOT rather than throughput:

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync<GetProduct, ProductDto>(new GetProduct(id), ct);
```

The streaming members stay untyped for now: their shape is under revision, and adding a surface
to something scheduled to change is work that has to be undone.

## Keep queries read-only

Nothing enforces it, and that is the point of having two modules — a query that writes is a
command that lied about itself. It also breaks reasonable assumptions: a caching interceptor on
the query pipeline is safe precisely because queries do not have effects.

## Dependencies

Constructor parameters come from the dispatching scope, like any other service. A read-only
`DbContext`, a Dapper connection factory, a cache — all ordinary registrations.

## Covariant handlers

A handler on a base query type serves the family, and a direct handler wins outright:

```csharp
public sealed class TenantQueryHandler<TResult> : IQueryHandler<TenantQuery<TResult>, TResult> { … }
```

Two handlers at the same level is `ERGO010`.

## Testing

```csharp
var handler = new GetProductHandler(new InMemoryProductRepository(seed));
var dto = await handler.HandleAsync(new GetProduct(id), new ErgosfareContext());
```

For a stream, drain it:

```csharp
var results = new List<ProductDto>();
await foreach (var item in handler.StreamAsync(query, new ErgosfareContext()))
    results.Add(item);
```
